b/ref 17592186045417] [17592186053390 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6bd68ceb-0543-55ca-92e6-971ba7ed329b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054581 #edb/ref 1069 "e-762" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054581 #edb/ref 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty { {{n}}(x-1)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=1$ of $f'(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c7dd3ad-eaa9-5312-adaf-3a0772e845f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054581 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty { {{n}}(x-1)^{n}} = (x-1) + {2(x-1)^2} + {3(x-1)^3} +\\cdots .\n$$\n\nTo find $f'(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+0+4(x-1)+9(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+4(x-1)+9(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054582 #edb/ref 1069 "e-763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054582 #edb/ref 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{ (x-3)^{n}}{n}\n$$\n\nFind $a_n,$ given that\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054582 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb7a106a-130a-5479-b60d-8ea76a8cd8de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054582 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n![](images/73/7316ce9632bc2ab0347accb71c4c0dc90db8a87e0f9bead704e0218523a9b367.png)\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n![](images/84/846a66292603d3436219826be2e9419b5798fa121341d1e1676fba63c0f37eec.png)\nWe can now write the Taylor series for $f'(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (x-3)^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\na_n = (x-3)^{n-1}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054583 #edb/ref 1069 "e-764" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054583 #edb/ref 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$  about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-3)^{n}\\,x^n}{n^3}\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x| <            R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054583 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e656ee8d-55b9-5ea6-beae-7c2caf329a6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054583 #edb/ref 1074 "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of $f'(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of $f'(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3}}{(-3)^{n}\\,{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)x\\cdot {n}^{3}}{(n+1)^{3}}| \\\\  & =3|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{3}} \\\\  & =3|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R = \\dfrac 1 3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054682 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054682 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054682 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054682 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054682 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054682 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186