 17592186055932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055649 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055649 #edb/ref 1061 2592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055649 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "24137bfa-62c0-5f75-81c4-d5f0a97c7f3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055649 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055650 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055650 #edb/ref 1061 2595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055650 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a87c1316-a593-50aa-a57c-45932bf5e5df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055650 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055651 #edb/ref 1061 2598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055651 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "db8d259a-9396-5153-933f-88b2dad6b30c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055651 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055932 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose the Taylor (Maclaurin) series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{2n+1} x^{2n+1}\n$$\n\nand converges to $f(x)$ for $|x| < R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. How can we calculate the first three non-zero terms of Taylor series for the derivative, $f'(x),$ about $x=0?$\n\nAll we need to do is differentiate the Taylor series of $f(x).$ Writing out the first three terms, we get\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{2n+1} x^{2n+1} = x - \\dfrac{x^3}{3} + \\dfrac{x^5}{5} - \\cdots.\n$$\n\nNow, to find $f'(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})-⋯ \\\\  & =1-{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! The radius of convergence of the Taylor series for $f'(x)$ is $R,$ the same as $f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055932 #edb/ref 1065 1318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055932 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "59207cd1-3d1f-52ac-9acb-354bc8dff029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055932 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319405 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319405 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319405 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03fe077c-2f54-5d1e-9d94-e81bf9e7195c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319716 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{n}{(2n+1)!}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319716 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319716 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "69ac432d-af48-5d58-9c4e-6b45fa9907cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320422 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186319405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320422 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320422 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320422 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186319405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320422 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7dfd9f6f-00ee-5561-ae17-a34a75446cf8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320546 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186319716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320546 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320546 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320546 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186319716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320546 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "baaed7d0-ce21-5a59-8834-a8eeeb951918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320991 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320991 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320991 #edb/ref 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{(2n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}\n$(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320991 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba1a2065-05f5-5d7a-9aeb-6e943be02b88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320991 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{120}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{(2n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{2x}{120}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}n{x}^{n-1}}{(2n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}}{(2n+1)!} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{(2n+1)!}(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321011 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321011 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352603" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321011 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321011 #edb/ref 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(4n-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$\n\nThe radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321011 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd3fd116-3eb9-5711-87df-f837fd78e515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321011 #edb/ref 1074 "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(4(n+1)-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n+1}}{(4n-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{4n+3}{4n-1}||\\frac{x}{4}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{4} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
